let's say we've got two countries  country a and country b  and at the start of our little  hypothetical thought experiment they  have a very stable exchange rate maybe  it's one for one every a  if you were to go into the into foreign  currency markets you could get a b for  it so there's kind of this  stable supply and demand between these  two currencies now let's say for  whatever reason folks in a start to  believe that b country b is the hot  place to invest they want to buy country  b's real estate they want to invest in  country b's stock market and so if they  want to buy stuff in b they essentially  have to hold more of b's currency and so  more and more people  more and more people in a want to  exchange their a's for country b's  currency so you have this huge supply of  a's coming onto the foreign exchange  markets but you still have the same  amount of b's that want to go that want  to go the other way maybe and you know  these are maybe there to either invest  in country a or maybe buy some of  country's a's exports or whatever it  might be but if we just let this happen  on its own where all of a sudden there's  a much larger demand for converting a's  into b then converting b's into a  you'll have a situation where the b's  will just get more expensive there's  more demand for b's than they are for  a's they'll get more expensive in terms  of a's so if you look at it from country  a's point of view you're now having to  pay more more a's  per b and a completely equivalent  statement from country b's perspective  you would now have to pay fewer  fewer  bees  fewer bees per a  now let's say for whatever reason you  are the central bank you are the central  bank right over here of  country b  and you don't like your currency  becoming stronger maybe you just don't  like the volatility in the exchange  markets you don't like the  the idea that the exchange rates go up  and down so dramatically maybe you just  don't want your exports to get expensive  or that imports from another country to  get cheap for whatever reason you do not  like this movement happening and so  you're what you're going to do is  because you have the right to do it you  you are the central bank of your country  you can print more of your currency  right here the currency b's  so you're going to print more of these  so you're  you could print more of them just like  that and then you can use that extra b  that you just printed to buy more a's  and so now you've kind of re-normalized  the supply and the demand for bees but  what's going to be the end product of  that well you did successfully keep your  currency from appreciating relative to  the a's which based on how this was all  set up that was your goal but the other  thing that you ended up with is since  you printed b's and you're able to buy  a's in the open market you now on your  balance sheet or maybe in your vaults  you have a bunch of a's and so when  people talk about foreign currency  reserves let me write it down foreign  currency reserves and pretty much every  bank every central bank has foreign  currency reserves this is what they are  talking about that central bank printed  their own currency and went out into  currency foreign exchange markets and  bought other countries currencies and  there's multiple reasons for them doing  it this might be one of them or it might  be to protect them in the case that this  whole dynamic reverses in the other  direction especially if it reverses in a  very dramatic way
 

